        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.194,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "TRIANGLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "79"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.139,
            0.388,
            0.159
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.3 Similarity of Triangles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.165,
            0.626,
            0.185
          ],
          "content": "What can you say about the similarity of two triangles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.191,
            0.9,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": "You may recall that triangle is also a polygon. So, we can state the same conditions for the similarity of two triangles. That is:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.236,
            0.442,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "Two triangles are similiar, if"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.261,
            0.609,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": "(i) their corresponding angles are equal and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.286,
            0.826,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "(ii) their corresponding sides are in the same ratio (or proportion)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.311,
            0.595,
            0.408
          ],
          "content": "Note that if corresponding angles of two triangles are equal, then they are known as equiangular triangles. A famous Greek mathematician Thales gave an important truth relating to two equiangular triangles which is as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.414,
            0.595,
            0.453
          ],
          "content": "The ratio of any two corresponding sides in two equiangular triangles is always the same."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.458,
            0.594,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": "It is believed that he had used a result called the Basic Proportionality Theorem (now known as the Thales Theorem) for the same."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.61,
            0.313,
            0.885,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.522,
            0.593,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "To understand the Basic Proportionality Theorem, let us perform the following activity:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.566,
            0.593,
            0.624
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 2 :** Draw any angle XAY and on its one arm AX, mark points (say five points) P, Q, D, R and B such that $\\mathrm{AP} = \\mathrm{PQ} = \\mathrm{QD} = \\mathrm{DR} = \\mathrm{RB}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.63,
            0.592,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "Now, through B, draw any line intersecting arm AY at C (see Fig. 6.9)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.61,
            0.578,
            0.868,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.675,
            0.594,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "Also, through the point D, draw a line parallel to BC to intersect AC at E. Do you observe from your constructions that $\\frac { \\mathrm { A D } } { \\mathrm { D B } }   =   \\frac { 3 } { 2 }   ?$ Measure AE and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.701,
            0.712,
            0.786,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 6.9"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.765,
            0.902,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": "EC. What about $\\frac { \\mathrm { A E } } { \\mathrm { E C } }$ ? Observe that $\\frac { \\mathrm { A E } } { \\mathrm { E C } }$ is also equal to $\\frac { 3 } { 2 }$ . Thus, you can see that in $\\Delta   \\mathrm { A B C }$ , DE || BC and $\\frac { \\mathrm { A D } } { \\mathrm { D B } }   =   \\frac { \\mathrm { A E } } { \\mathrm { E C } }$ . Is it a coincidence? No, it is due to the following theorem (known as the Basic Proportionality Theorem):"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
